WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.569
A customer calls support about a coffee.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:10.948
The support screen asks the database for every payment made with card 4242.

3
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:15.888
The table holds about a million payments, and 14 of them are this card's.

4
00:00:15.888 --> 00:00:19.601
How much does the database have to read to find those 14?

5
00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:20.839
Hold that question.

6
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:23.820
Start with a cookbook of six hundred pages.

7
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.441
Which recipes use lemons?

8
00:00:25.441 --> 00:00:27.709
You could turn every page and look.

9
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:33.060
Or you could open the index at the back, find lemon, and read off six page numbers.

10
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.777
Now ask the same book for every recipe that takes under 20 minutes.

11
00:00:37.777 --> 00:00:39.607
The index is no help at all.

12
00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:43.265
It's sorted by ingredient, and cooking time isn't in it.

13
00:00:43.265 --> 00:00:45.160
Keep that one in your pocket.

14
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.218
A database table is a book too.

15
00:00:48.218 --> 00:00:56.672
PostgreSQL, a free database program, keeps it in pages of 8 KB, the block it reads at a time.

16
00:00:56.672 --> 00:01:01.399
This table holds 1,000,478 payments in 23,818 pages.

17
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.351
Each new row goes wherever there's room, in no particular order.

18
00:01:06.351 --> 00:01:10.360
So card 4242's 14 payments are spread across the file.

19
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.667
With nothing else to go on, there's one way to find them: read every page, and test every row.

20
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:18.664
That's called a sequential scan.

21
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:28.075
PostgreSQL's report of the run says three processes shared the work, and each threw away 333,488 rows.

22
00:01:28.075 --> 00:01:33.330
That's 23,818 pages for 14 rows, in a quarter of a second.

23
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.411
And the cost grows with the table.

24
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:40.233
It's reading the whole cookbook every time someone wants lemonade.

25
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:45.613
What we want is the cookbook's trick: a list at the back that jumps straight to the right pages.

26
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:51.110
First, the obvious idea: why not keep the table itself sorted by card number?

27
00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:57.101
Because a table can be in only one order, and support asks by card, by shop and by date.

28
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:03.360
Keeping it sorted would also mean moving rows to make room whenever a payment lands in the middle.

29
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.492
So PostgreSQL leaves the table in arrival order, and builds a separate sorted copy.

30
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:10.188
That copy is the index.

31
00:02:10.188 --> 00:02:14.551
The cookbook's index lists every ingredient in order, with its pages.

32
00:02:14.551 --> 00:02:19.356
Ours lists every card number in order, with the address of each of its rows.

33
00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:24.998
A row's address is its page and its slot on that page.

34
00:02:24.998 --> 00:02:31.033
Card 4242's first payment is at 339, 35: row 35 on page 339.

35
00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:34.755
PostgreSQL calls this address a ctid.

36
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:40.413
A million sorted card numbers still fill 1,130 pages, called leaves.

37
00:02:40.413 --> 00:02:42.764
That's still a lot to search.

38
00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:52.724
So above every 283 or so leaves, PostgreSQL adds an inner page that says which card number each leaf starts with, and one root page above those.

39
00:02:52.724 --> 00:02:58.959
A big book's index does the same with letter headings: you find L before you look for lemon.

40
00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:09.231
The result is a B+tree, a tree of pages where each level narrows the search: one root, 4 inner pages and 1,130 leaves.

41
00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:13.104
So how many pages does card 4242 cost now?

42
00:03:13.104 --> 00:03:14.503
Pause and guess.

43
00:03:17.767 --> 00:03:21.838
One walk down the tree: the root, an inner page and a leaf.

44
00:03:21.838 --> 00:03:25.426
That's 3 pages, and the leaf holds the 14 addresses.

45
00:03:25.426 --> 00:03:28.048
Then the 14 table pages they point to.

46
00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:31.149
17 pages in all, instead of 23,818.

47
00:03:31.149 --> 00:03:38.643
A quarter of a millisecond instead of a quarter of a second: about a thousand times faster.

48
00:03:38.643 --> 00:03:42.431
Same 14 rows, same coffee, a lot less reading.

49
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:44.670
Now, the question in your pocket.

50
00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.430
This index is sorted by card.

51
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:53.652
Ask for payments by anything else, and its order can't help, just as the cookbook's index couldn't find a quick dinner.

52
00:03:53.867 --> 00:03:55.550
And a copy has a cost.

53
00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.992
This one takes 9 MB, next to the table's 186.

54
00:03:58.992 --> 00:04:05.876
Every new payment goes into it too, so indexes make reads cheap and writes more expensive.

55
00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:13.135
So the moral of the story: an index is a sorted copy of a few columns, each entry pointing to its row.

56
00:04:13.135 --> 00:04:18.008
A query is fast when it can find one short run of entries in that copy.

57
00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:19.384
One quick check.

58
00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:22.667
Support now asks for every payment at one shop.

59
00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:25.112
Does the index on card number help?

60
00:04:25.112 --> 00:04:26.229
Pause and think.

61
00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:29.664
No.

62
00:04:29.664 --> 00:04:33.863
One shop's payments are spread all over an index sorted by card.

63
00:04:33.863 --> 00:04:38.063
It's the quick dinner again, so it's back to reading every page.
